.TH STRING 3
.SH NAME
strcat, strncat, strcmp, strncmp, strcpy, strncpy, strlen, strchr, strrchr,
strstr, strtok, strdup, index, rindex, memcpy, memmove, memset, memcmp, memchr
\- string and memory operations
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <string.h>
.PP
.B char *strcat(char *s1, const char *s2);
.PP
.B char *strncat(char *s1, const char *s2, size_t n);
.PP
.B int strcmp(const char *s1, const char *s2);
.PP
.B int strncmp(const char *s1, const char *s2, size_t n);
.PP
.B char *strcpy(char *s1, const char *s2);
.PP
.B char *strncpy(char *s1, const char *s2, size_t n);
.PP
.B size_t strlen(const char *s);
.PP
.B char *strchr(const char *s, int c);
.PP
.B char *strrchr(const char *s, int c);
.PP
.B char *strstr(const char *s, const char *sub);
.PP
.B char *strtok(char *s, const char *sep);
.PP
.B char *strdup(const char *s);
.PP
.B char *index(const char *s, int c);
.PP
.B char *rindex(const char *s, int c);
.PP
.B void *memcpy(void *d, const void *s, size_t n);
.PP
.B void *memmove(void *d, const void *s, size_t n);
.PP
.B void *memset(void *d, int c, size_t n);
.PP
.B int memcmp(const void *a, const void *b, size_t n);
.PP
.B void *memchr(const void *s, int c, size_t n);
.SH DESCRIPTION
These functions operate on null-terminated strings.
They do not check for overflow of any receiving string.
.PP
.I Strcat
appends a copy of string
.I s2
to the end of string
.IR s1 .
.I Strncat
copies at most
.I n
characters.
Both return a pointer to the null-terminated result.
.PP
.I Strcmp
compares its arguments and returns an integer
greater than, equal to, or less than 0,
according as
.I s1
is lexicographically greater than, equal to, or
less than
.IR s2 .
.I Strncmp
makes the same comparison but looks at at most
.I n
characters.
.PP
.I Strcpy
copies string
.I s2
to
.I s1,
stopping after the null character has been moved.
.I Strncpy
copies exactly
.I n
characters,
truncating or null-padding
.I s2;
the target may not be null-terminated if the length
of
.I s2
is
.I n
or more.
Both return
.IR s1 .
.PP
.I Strlen
returns the number of non-null characters in
.IR s .
.PP
.I Index
.RI ( rindex )
returns a pointer to the first (last)
occurrence of character
.I c
in string
.I s,
or zero if
.I c
does not occur in  the string.
.PP
.B Note:
everything from here down is C11 and is not on v7's page.
.I Strchr
and
.I strrchr
are the standard spellings of
.I index
and
.IR rindex ,
and all four are present.
.I Strstr
returns a pointer to the first occurrence of the string
.I sub
in
.IR s ,
or NULL.
.I Strtok
splits
.I s
into tokens separated by any character of
.IR sep ,
returning one per call \- the first call names the string, the rest pass NULL \-
and it writes on the string it is given.
.PP
.B Note:
.I strdup
is neither v7's nor C11's \- it is POSIX's, and C took it only in C23.  It
returns a
.IR malloc (3)ed
copy of
.IR s ,
which the caller frees, or NULL if the allocation failed.  It is the one routine
on this page that allocates, and therefore the one that can fail.
.PP
.I Memcpy
copies
.I n
bytes,
.I memmove
does the same for overlapping regions,
.I memset
fills
.I n
bytes with
.IR c ,
.I memcmp
compares
.I n
bytes, and
.I memchr
finds the first
.I c
in
.I n
bytes.
.PP
.B Note:
<string.h> declares all of these, including
.I index
and
.IR rindex .
v7 declared none of them anywhere and left them to K&R's implicit
.BR int ,
which would have made
.I strcpy
return an integer.
.SH SEE ALSO
strtol(3), atof(3), malloc(3) for
.I strdup's
storage
.SH BUGS
.B Note:
v7's bug \- "strcmp uses native character comparison, which is signed on PDP11's,
unsigned on other machines" \- is settled here rather than left open.
.B char is unsigned,
so the comparison is unsigned, and
.I strcmp
of two strings differing above 0177 orders them by their byte values.
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.SS These are the c-compiler's, not v7's
The whole
.IR str* / mem*
family and
.IR atoi (3)
are taken from the external c-compiler's own libc rather than ported from v7,
because they were already written for this machine's fat
.B char *
and one-word scalars, and their signatures already match the <string.h> this tree
supplies \- so nothing ends up defined as
.B "int n"
where the declaration says
.BR size_t .
Each file says at its head where it came from, and a divergence has to be written
down there.
.SS A relational between two char * is safe here, and it is used
A fat pointer does
.I not
sort as a plain word \- incrementing one
.I decreases
its 3-bit byte offset, which sits above the word address \- so a raw word compare
of two pointers into the same word comes out backwards.
.PP
.B The compiler knows.
It lowers a relational between two pointers it can see are fat through
.BR b$pdiff ,
the same helper as
.BR \- ,
and tests the sign.
.IR Memmove 's
direction test depends on that, and so does
.IR qsort (3)'s
partition.  lib/test/strings overlaps its arguments
.I within one word
on purpose, to keep it that way.
.PP
.B That is not a general licence.
Where the comparison is between a pointer and something the front end does not
carry as a fat pointer, it silently reduces to an integer comparison of the whole
word and the byte offset dominates.  See
.IR getpass (3),
where exactly that made the routine return the empty string every time.
.SS strlen returns a signed size_t
.B size_t
is signed here, a deliberate departure from C11 sec 7.19 recorded in
include/README.md, so a difference of two lengths behaves in a mixed comparison.
